Time Series Forecasting Project Report
Demand Forecasting for French Bakery Sales

Project Overview

This project focuses on building robust time-series forecasting models to predict daily product-level demand for a French bakery. The goal was to support operational decision-making across production planning, inventory management, and resource allocation.

The project applies ARIMA and SARIMA statistical forecasting models, performs rolling cross-validation for reliability, and incorporates exogenous variables such as price and engineered time-based features. It also includes a fully automated forecasting workflow covering preprocessing, model training, model selection, cross-validation, and reporting.

Dataset Description

Source: Daily sales data for multiple bakery products

Key Fields:

unique_id – product name

ds – date

y – daily sales quantity

unit_price – product price

Products with at least 28 days of data were retained for stability.

Exploratory Time-Series Analysis

Trend & Seasonality Insights

Visual inspection revealed weekly seasonality, especially for products like BAGUETTE and CROISSANT.

Sales patterns were inspected using:

Rolling windows

Fourier/time-based features

Seasonal plots

These patterns informed model choices and the use of SARIMA with weekly season length (=7).

Baseline Modeling

To establish benchmark performance, the following simple models were built:

Naive Forecast

Historic Average

Window Average (7-day window)

Seasonal Naive (weekly seasonality)

Evaluation on 7-day holdout:

Model	MAE
Naive	6.11
Historic Average	5.23
Window Average	5.01
Seasonal Naive	4.61 (Best baseline)

This provided a minimum performance standard for ARIMA-based models.

ARIMA & SARIMA Modeling

Models Built

ARIMA (AutoARIMA, non-seasonal)

SARIMA (AutoARIMA with season_length=7)

These were applied to selected products (BAGUETTE, CROISSANT).

Performance on Holdout Data

Model	MAE
ARIMA	11.96
SARIMA	8.90 (best overall)
Naive	17.31
Seasonal Naive	12.71

SARIMA effectively captured weekly seasonality and provided the most operationally stable forecasts.

Rolling Cross-Validation
(Ensuring Forecast Stability, Reliability & Operational Readiness)

To ensure the models generalize beyond a single test window, 8 rolling windows of cross-validation were performed.

Models Compared

Seasonal Naive

ARIMA

SARIMA

CV Performance (Mean MAE across windows)

Model	MAE
Seasonal Naive	21.12
ARIMA	21.23
SARIMA	19.28 (best performing model)

Rolling CV confirmed that SARIMA remained the most stable and reliable model across different forecasting windows.

Forecasting with Exogenous Features (SARIMAX)

To improve forecast accuracy, exogenous predictors were introduced:

Exogenous Variables Used

Unit price

Fourier seasonal features

Calendar features: day, week, month

Models Built

SARIMA with price exogenous

SARIMA with Fourier/time features

Impact on Cross-Validation MAE

Model	MAE
SARIMA (no exogenous)	19.28
SARIMA + price	19.21
SARIMA + Fourier/time features	19.53

SARIMA with price data performed the best, confirming that price changes influence demand and improve forecast accuracy.

Prediction Intervals

To support risk-aware planning, 80% prediction intervals were generated using AutoARIMA. This helps determine:

Minimum production levels

Inventory buffers

Uncertainty-aware staffing decisions

Automated End-to-End Forecasting Workflow

A fully automated pipeline was implemented covering:

Data preprocessing

Filtering by minimum time-series length

Handling missing values

Generating time-based features

Model training

AutoARIMA & AutoSARIMA for automatic hyperparameter selection

Rolling cross-validation

Automated multi-window backtesting

Forecast generation

With and without exogenous features

Future exogenous preparation

Evaluation & visualization

MAE scoring

Comparison bar charts

Forecast plots with historical data

This workflow ensures repeatability, scalability, and easy integration into production systems.

Key Outcomes

SARIMA consistently delivered the most accurate and stable forecasts across baselines, holdout tests, and rolling CV.

Inclusion of price as an exogenous feature improved accuracy further.

Clear weekly seasonality identified across all major products.

A fully automated forecasting framework was built, enabling data-driven planning for a retail bakery environment.

Skills Demonstrated (Resume-Ready)

Time Series Forecasting (ARIMA, SARIMA, SARIMAX)

Rolling Cross-Validation

Feature Engineering for Time Series (Fourier, calendar features)

Exogenous modeling

Automated ML pipelines

Evaluation (MAE, backtesting)

Data Visualization and Reporting

Python (Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib), StatsForecast library

Conclusion

This project delivered a comprehensive, production-ready time-series forecasting solution capable of improving operational planning for a retail bakery. Through statistical modeling, cross-validation, and exogenous analysis, the pipeline provides accurate, stable, and actionable forecasts that align with real-world business needs.